= Subsumption classification: this branch against upstream, 2026-08-04 =

Written BEFORE the rebase and before any further code, as ordered. It is what
stops a blind rebase from silently discarding an upstream fix or reintroducing a
bug upstream already closed.

Bases
  merge-base   8b8cc7d  "fix(watch): keep supervision alive across delivered wakes and parked lanes (#3)"
  synced main  806e05b  verified by hash against the captain's stated value
  divergence   this branch +13, origin/main +25 from the merge-base

Overlap set. The captain's order named six seam files. The set of files changed
by BOTH sides is twelve; the extra six are listed and classified too, because a
rebase does not care which list a file was on.

  SEAM (named)                        ALSO OVERLAPPING (not named)
  bin/fm-watch.sh                     bin/fm-session-lock-lib.sh
  bin/fm-watch-arm.sh                 bin/fm-test-run.sh
  bin/fm-wake-lib.sh                  AGENTS.md
  docs/watcher-continuity.md          docs/configuration.md
  tests/fm-watcher-lock.test.sh       docs/scripts.md
  bin/fm-claude-stop-autoarm.sh       docs/verification/supervision.md
    (named, but NOT in the overlap    .agents/skills/harness-adapters/SKILL.md
     set - this branch never touched
     it; classified anyway)

===============================================================================
FIRST: two of the round's leads do not survive verification
===============================================================================
Both were given as leads, not conclusions, with instructions to verify. Neither
holds, and the same evidence check kills both.

LEAD: "Upstream reordered continuity to Option B - fm-watch-arm.sh now owns a
harness-independent floor that arms and verifies one detached successor BEFORE
delivering the wake."

  NOT UPSTREAM'S. That text and that behavior are already present at the
  merge-base, in docs/watcher-continuity.md and in bin/fm-watch-arm.sh. They
  arrived with the fork's own #3, the commit this branch was cut from.
      git show 8b8cc7d:docs/watcher-continuity.md | grep -c "harness-independent floor"   -> 1
      git diff 8b8cc7d origin/main -- docs/watcher-continuity.md | grep -c "^+.*harness-independent floor" -> 0
  The upstream diff adds neither the phrase nor the mechanism.

LEAD: "Your Part 1 prime suspect may be subsumed - docs/watcher-continuity.md NOW
states explicitly that a forked session leaves state/.lock naming the still-live
pre-fork harness, so the hook stays inert by design."

  ALSO NOT UPSTREAM'S, and the word "now" is doing false work. That sentence is
  likewise already at the merge-base and is not added by the upstream diff:
      git show 8b8cc7d:docs/watcher-continuity.md | grep -c "forked session leaves"       -> 1
      git diff 8b8cc7d origin/main -- docs/watcher-continuity.md | grep -c "^+.*forked session leaves" -> 0
  Reading the post-sync file and attributing pre-existing fork text to upstream is
  how both leads formed. Upstream additionally leaves the gate itself untouched:
  bin/fm-claude-stop-autoarm.sh's fm_session_lock_owned_by_self / stale-owner
  recovery block is not modified by the upstream diff at all.

  So Part 1's finding is NOT subsumed, and there is nothing to drop. It was also
  never the finding those leads describe: that forked hooks are inert was already
  recorded in the incident doc before this lane started. Part 1's contribution was
  deciding WHICH of three stated hypotheses actually caused the duplication - by
  probe, with the pre-fix capture preserved - and upstream touches none of that.

This is the reason the order says leads are not conclusions. Recorded here so the
next reader does not re-derive it, and so the classification below is trusted on
evidence rather than on who wrote what.

