PER-FILE CLASSIFICATION
===============================================================================

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
bin/fm-wake-lib.sh - CONFLICTING (one field, two vocabularies) + COMPLEMENTARY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Upstream added:
  - FM_WATCHER_MATCHED_IDENTITY / FM_WATCHER_HEALTHY_IDENTITY, publishing the
    matched lock identity to callers.
  - fm_lock_set_role / fm_lock_role, plus "role" in fm_lock_clean_known_files.
  - fm_failure_episode_reset, the positive recovery reset.

This branch added: the supervisor singleton (fm_supervisor_singleton_acquire,
fm_singleton_holder_verified, fm_singleton_reclaim_reused, fm_singleton_seed),
the owner-seed seam, fm_pid_is_zombie, and "role" + "state" in
fm_lock_clean_known_files.

CONFLICTING - the `role` field. Both sides introduced a file named `role` inside
the shared lock primitive, with disjoint vocabularies and opposite write
disciplines:
    upstream : values autoarm|terminal-check, written by fm_lock_set_role AFTER
               acquisition, by the holder, only on state/.claude-autoarm.lock,
               and rejects any value outside its enum
    this lane: values watcher|supervise-daemon|pr-check-migration, written into
               the owner directory BEFORE the symlink publishes the lock, on
               .watch.lock and .supervise-daemon.lock
They do not collide at runtime today - different locks, and nothing reads
fm_lock_role on the watch lock - but this is one field name with two owners in
one shared primitive, which is the shape this fleet already knows produces silent
failures. fm_lock_clean_known_files is genuinely shared and both sides edited it.

  WHICH IS BETTER, by the property protected: they protect different properties
  and neither should be dropped. Upstream's answers "which of two co-operating
  Claude mechanisms holds this owner lock", set after the fact. This lane's
  answers "what kind of supervisor is this, published atomically with the lock so
  a peer can never observe a lock it cannot identify" - the before-publish timing
  is load-bearing for singleton-at-birth and cannot be moved to a setter.
  RESOLUTION: keep both behaviors, remove the name collision. This lane renames
  its field to `supervisor-role` and leaves `role` to upstream's enum, since
  upstream's is the narrower, already-validated vocabulary and its setter would
  reject this lane's values anyway. Both entries stay in the shared cleanup.

COMPLEMENTARY - FM_WATCHER_*_IDENTITY. Independent of the singleton and useful
to it; keep upstream's as-is. No change needed on either side.

COMPLEMENTARY, with a shared principle - fm_failure_episode_reset. See the Part 4
section below; it is the most consequential item in this classification.

